#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""know - a knowledge cache over the OS/2 library: what was established, what
was searched for and NOT found, and a full-text index of the material itself.

WHY THIS EXISTS. A session asks the same questions of the same few hundred
megabytes of IBM and community material, from cold, every time -- and the
expensive half of that work is never written down anywhere a later session can
read. This store holds three things:

    chunks    the ingested material, searchable by words rather than by the
              exact token a grep would need;
    facts     what a session established, each with a citation and a command
              that re-checks it;
    searches  what was looked for, where, and whether it was there -- INCLUDING
              the negatives.

THE NEGATIVES ARE THE PART A GREP CANNOT GIVE YOU. A grep that returns nothing
cannot distinguish "this is not documented" from "nobody has ever looked".
Recording "searched X, absent, stop looking" converts an open-ended search into
a closed question, and it is expensive to produce and free to keep
(CONTRIBUTING.md section 6: negative results are contributions).

WHAT IT IS NOT. Not an authority, and not a replacement for reading the source.
It is OURS: it holds what previous sessions concluded, and previous sessions
were sometimes wrong.

    RETRIEVAL TELLS YOU WHERE TO LOOK AND WHAT WAS CONCLUDED.
    ANYTHING LOAD-BEARING IS RE-OPENED IN THE PRIMARY SOURCE BEFORE IT LANDS.

FTS5 ONLY, DELIBERATELY. Measured over 33,384 real chunks of this kind of
material: a porter-stemmed, heading-weighted FTS5 index scored 16/25 at k=5
against a 768-dimension embedding hybrid's 14/25, at half the latency. It also
always works -- FTS5 is compiled into the SQLite that ships with every stock
Python, while an embedding column is silently model-versioned: written by a
different model it does not fail, it returns wrong neighbours. That is the
silent-wrong class, and it is invisible to any test that only asks whether a
query returned something.

STORE LOCATION, first hit wins:

    $OS2_KNOW_DB  ->  ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/os2-toolkit/know.db

It is deliberately NOT inside the OS/2 library. The chunks are re-ingestable --
throw them away and `know ingest` rebuilds them from the material. The facts and
the negative results are not: nothing regenerates them but the sessions that
paid for them. So the store is not disposable, it must survive the library being
moved, rebuilt or emptied, and it has to work on a machine with no library
configured at all.

Verbs (run with no arguments for the summary):
    init                       create/upgrade the store
    ingest [KIND] [PATH...]    index material; with no paths, from the library
    q QUERY                    search facts + material, and report prior searches
    add                        record a derived fact (citation REQUIRED)
    absent                     record a negative result as a finding
    show ID                    one fact (12) or negative (neg7) in full
    log QUESTION               the search transcript for a question
    supersede OLD BY -m WHY    correct a fact (12) or a negative (neg7)
    promote ID --prov ...      raise a provenance tag (human/review only)
    stale                      source files changed since ingest
    refresh [--prune]          re-ingest what changed
    verify [ID|--all]          re-run stored verify_cmd's; exit = DISAGREE count
    export DIR                 readable prose + a LOSSLESS know.jsonl
    import PATH                rebuild facts/searches from that jsonl
    import --merge PATH        add another store's export, under new ids
    stats
"""
